	
	💡 Question: Inter Thread Communication — ATM Simulation
	📘 Problem Statement
	
	Develop a Java program using Inter Thread Communication (ITC) concepts where multiple threads (Depositor, Drawer, and BalanceChecker) interact with a common Account object.
	
	The program should demonstrate:
	
	wait() and notify() usage
	
	Synchronized methods for thread safety
	
	Real-time coordination between deposit and withdrawal actions
	
	Thread communication over a shared balance
	
	@ Requirements
	
	1. Create a class called Account
	Instance Variable:
	private int balance = 0; (initial balance is 0)	
	Methods:
	1️⃣ deposit(int amount)
	Make it a synchronized method.
	Print which thread is depositing how much.
	Add the amount to the balance.
	Display the updated balance.
	Use notify() to wake up any thread waiting to withdraw.
	
	2# withdraw(int amount)	
	Make it a synchronized method.
	Print which thread is trying to withdraw and how much.
	If the current balance is less than the withdrawal amount,
	use a while loop and call wait() to make the thread wait until there is enough balance.
	Once sufficient balance is available, deduct the amount and print the updated balance.
	Use notify() to inform other waiting threads.
	
	2. Create a class Drawer (extends Thread)
	Instance Variable:
	private Account account;
	Constructor:
	Use a parameterized constructor to assign the Account object.
	
	run() method:
	
	Create an int[] withdrawals = {1000, 2000, 1500, 500}
	
	Loop through the array using a for-each loop.
	
	For each value, call the withdraw() method.
	
	Add a 1-second delay (Thread.sleep(1000)) after each withdrawal.
	
	3. Create a class Depositor (extends Thread)
	
	Instance Variable:
	
	private Account account;
	
	Constructor:
	
	Use a parameterized constructor to assign the Account object.
	
	run() method:
	
	Create an int[] deposits = {2000, 1000, 3000}
	
	Loop through the array using a for-each loop.
	
	For each value, call the deposit() method.
	
	Add a 1.2-second delay (Thread.sleep(1200)) after each deposit.
	
	4. Create a class BalanceChecker (extends Thread)
	
	Instance Variable:
	
	private Account account;
	
	Constructor:
	
	Initialize it using a parameterized constructor.
	
	run() method:
	
	In a loop (5 times), print the message "Checking balance..."
	
	Add a short delay (e.g., 800 ms) between each check.
	
	5. Create an ELC class named InterThreadCommunicationDemo
	
	Inside the main() method:
	
	Create an Account object.
	
	Create objects of Drawer, Depositor, and BalanceChecker, passing the same Account object to all (HAS-A relation).
	
	Start all threads to see the wait–notify coordination in action.
	
	🧩 Expected Behavior
	
	If the Drawer tries to withdraw but there isn’t enough balance → it waits.
	
	When the Depositor adds money → it notifies the Drawer to continue.
	
	The BalanceChecker runs independently to display balance check attempts.
	
	All threads share the same Account safely because of synchronized methods.